  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nA closed upper low is departing the region as increasing west\nwinds wrap around the system into NE Montana for Monday. These\nwinds will ensure that the Lake Wind Advisory for Fort Peck Lake\nremains well on track. Meanwhile, closer to the low, wind gusts\ncould exceed 40 to 50 mph along the Canadian border where pressure\ngradient is tightest. An isolated high wind gust cannot be ruled \nout east of Opheim to the North Dakota border. Given low\nprobability and coverage expected, no high wind highlights were\nconsidered at this time but this merits watching.\n\nMeanwhile, upper riding will move into the region for warm and dry\nweather conditions on Tuesday as high temperatures soar well into\nthe 80s. For the middle of the week, the ridge axis will shift\ndownstream with upper level diffluent flow setting up over the\nNorthern High Plains. This will mean additional thunderstorm\nchances by Wednesday evening.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNBM was left untouched for this forecast cycle as it captured\nlarge scale patterns well. Much of the wind is expected to be\ndriven synoptically so opted not to add in high res data at this\ntime. To that end, there is low confidence that a remote shower\nnear the Canadian border east of Opheim could mix down a near high\nwind Monday afternoon.\n\nOtherwise, ensembles show excellent consensus on the ridge\ndeveloping on Tuesday before quickly translating downstream,\nsetting the stage for unsettled weather to return for the middle\nof the week.",
